From 185d8cd7d055c0d0a0b6f86bf88a677a58b6f2ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "D.R.racer" <drracer@seznam.cz>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 21:57:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/sun4i: backend: expose the hardware cursor as a cursor
 plane
To: Chen-Yu Tsai <wens@kernel.org>,
    Maxime Ripard <mripard@kernel.org>
Cc: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>,
    Samuel Holland <samuel@sholland.org>,
    Maarten Lankhorst <maarten.lankhorst@linux.intel.com>,
    Thomas Zimmermann <tzimmermann@suse.de>,
    David Airlie <airlied@gmail.com>,
    Simona Vetter <simona@ffwll.ch>,
    dri-devel@lists.freedesktop.org,
    linux-arm-kernel@lists.infradead.org,
    linux-sunxi@lists.linux.dev,
    linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org

The A10/A20 display backend has a hardware cursor unit of its own,
separate from its four layers and drawn above them: 32x32 pixels at 8
bits per pixel through a palette of 256 ARGB8888 colours (or larger
cursors at fewer bits per pixel). sun4i-drm does not use it, so X falls
back to a software cursor and redraws it on every mouse move; on a
Cubietruck that is enough to make video playback miss frames while the
mouse moves. The vendor 3.4 stack (fbturbo) used the unit.

Expose it as a 32x32 ARGB8888 cursor plane. A new image is converted
into palette and indices when it changes (straight alpha, index 0
transparent; an image with more than 255 colours loses low bits per
channel until it fits); a move only rewrites the position registers.
A cursor partly above or left of the screen uses the unit's start
offsets. Moving a visible cursor with the same image is an async plane
update (written immediately, latched at the next vblank); the async
path is not taken while another commit on the CRTC is still being
programmed.

Cursor-only commits must not involve the layers. The backend's
atomic_check skips the layer check for them (otherwise every layer of
the CRTC is added to the state, which rules out the single-plane async
update and reprograms all layers, frontend included, on every move),
and atomic_begin does not wait for the previous register load (one
vblank per move, which would block the X server in the cursor ioctl
with a 125 Hz mouse).

The plane has an immutable zpos above the layers so that zpos
normalisation keeps the layers' order, and the backend's layer check
skips it. The DRM cursor size is set to 32x32 so that userspace sizes
its cursors accordingly; that hint is set for every SoC sun4i-drm
drives, but only matters where a cursor plane exists.

Register layout from the A20 user manual (DEBE_HWCCTL_REG,
DEBE_HWCFBCTL_REG, HWC pattern memory and palette). The plane is only
created for the A10 and A20 backends; the other SoCs sun4i-drm drives
are left alone.

Tested on a Cubietruck (A20, 2 GiB) with 6.18.56 under Xorg, with
HDMI and VGA (out-of-tree VGA and VDPAU patches): X uses the cursor
plane on both monitors. With the software cursor, a 1080p25 video
showed 155-196 late frames per 20 s while the mouse moved (56 with the
mouse still); with this patch a 1080p50 video drops 22 of 999 frames
while the mouse moves (12 with the mouse still). Not tested on A10.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
Tested on Armbian's 6.18.56. There sun4i still had its own
atomic_check, so the patch also made it take the async decision for
legacy cursor updates. On v7.3-rc7 sun4i uses drm_atomic_helper_check(),
which already does that, so that hunk was dropped. The v7.3-rc7
version also creates the plane only on the A10/A20 backends (new
has_hwc quirk; the tested version created it on every backend, which
on the A20 is the same), and sun4i_crtc_cursor_only() now also
requires the CRTC's cursor plane to be in the state, so that a commit
without any plane on the CRTC still waits for the register load. The
v7.3-rc7 version is build-tested only (W=1).

Open question: the A20 manual does not say whether the HWC registers
are latched by the backend's register buffer like the layer registers.
The cursor works as written: the registers are written, then loaded
with LOADCTL.

The patch was written with an AI assistant; I reviewed and tested it.

